response:
To find the remainder of $2x^6-x^4+4x^2-7$ upon division by $x^2+4x+3$, we just need to see what, in $x^2+4x+3$, we can substitute $x \rightarrow$ to equal to $2x^6-x^4+4x^2-7$. Notice that
\begin{align*}
x^2+4x+3 &= x^2+4x+3 \\
&= ((x+2)^2-1)\\
&= (x+2-1)(x+2+1) \\
&= (x+1)(x+3)
\end{align*}
So when $x \rightarrow -1$, $(x+1)=0$ and similarly $(x+3)=0$, and the original sentonence into $(-1)^2+4(-1)+3 = 1-4+3 = 0$.
And when $x \rightarrow -3$, $(-3)^2+4(-3)+3 = 9-12+3 = 0$. So we substitute $x$ by $-1$ and $-3$ to get
\begin{align*}
2x^6-x^4+4x^2-7 \rightarrow \\
2(-1)^6-(-1)^4+4(-1)^2-7 = 2-1+4-7 = -2 \\
2x^6-x^4+4x^2-7 \rightarrow \\
2(-3)^6-(-3)^4+4(-3)^2-7 = 2\cdot729-81 + 108 - 7 = 1458-72 = 1386 
\end{align*}
So the divisor $x^2+4x+3$ actually creates 0,2,1386 when $x$ equals to $-1,-1,-3$. So the remainder we are looking for is 2. We have completely used the mental power we have so far! Now why doesn't the remainder actually exist??? I will only tell you the trick. The remainder has to be an integer, between $0$ to $x^2+4x+3$, so it can not be 2 whole numbers. And we still have another useful clue from the code it self, that it behave as IF ELSE!!. What the formula can give you before the IF ELSE happens is the step before the remainder comes. In this case, we can calculate the remainder as $(18)(2)+(-2) = 34$. However, since the remainder must be between $0$ and $x^2+4x+3$, it is impossible:
\begin{align*}
x^2+4x+3=0
\end{align*}
and
\begin{align*}
34 \neq 0
\end{align*}
The Answer: There is no solution. [/think]

